  "intro": "A vector has magnitude and direction. In components, $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle a,b\\rangle$ (plane) or $\\langle a,b,c\\rangle$ (space). Magnitude $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$ (add $c^2$ in space). Add componentwise; scale by multiplying each component. The dot product $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}=u_1v_1+u_2v_2(+u_3v_3)$ gives $\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|\\cos\\theta$; it is zero exactly when the vectors are perpendicular. A unit vector is $\\frac{\\mathbf{v}}{\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|}$.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "For $\\mathbf{u}=\\langle 3,4\\rangle$, find $\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|$ and a unit vector in its direction.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Magnitude: $\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=\\sqrt{25}=5$.",
          "Unit vector: divide by the magnitude, $\\frac{1}{5}\\langle 3,4\\rangle$.",
          "So $\\hat{\\mathbf{u}}=\\left\\langle\\frac{3}{5},\\frac{4}{5}\\right\\rangle$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|=5$; $\\hat{\\mathbf{u}}=\\left\\langle\\frac{3}{5},\\frac{4}{5}\\right\\rangle$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "Find $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}$ and the angle for $\\mathbf{u}=\\langle 1,2\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 3,-1\\rangle$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Dot product: $1(3)+2(-1)=3-2=1$.",
          "Magnitudes: $\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|=\\sqrt{5}$, $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|=\\sqrt{10}$.",
          "$\\cos\\theta=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{10}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{50}}$, so $\\theta=\\arccos\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{50}}\\approx 81.9^\\circ$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}=1$; $\\theta\\approx 81.9^\\circ$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "Are $\\mathbf{u}=\\langle 2,3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 3,-2\\rangle$ perpendicular?",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Compute the dot product: $2(3)+3(-2)=6-6=0$.",
          "A zero dot product means the vectors are perpendicular."
        ],
        "answer": "Yes; their dot product is $0$."
      }
    },
